decisions:
  - id: D-symphony-naming
    decision: Renamed A-Mail → The Score, mail → symphony, mailbox → part, message → note
    rationale: 'The orchestral metaphor is load-bearing: score=shared document, notes=atomic expressions, parts=per-performer views. Musical naming reinforces the collaborative intelligence framing.'
  - id: D-fire-and-forget-bridge
    decision: Event bridge to Sentinel uses fire-and-forget POST with silent error swallowing
    rationale: Sentinel is optional infrastructure. Symphony must work without it. The JSONL files are the source of truth; Sentinel visualization is a nice-to-have.
  - id: D-remove-default-params
    decision: Removed default parameter values for @MainActor types in SwiftUI inits
    rationale: Swift 6 strict concurrency prevents calling @MainActor init() from nonisolated SwiftUI init contexts. Callers must explicitly pass instances.
  - id: D-backward-compat-migration
    decision: Auto-rename ~/.paradigm/mail/ → ~/.paradigm/score/ on first access via migrateFromLegacyMail()
    rationale: Users who installed Phase 0 yesterday shouldn't lose their mailboxes. One-time rename is invisible and idempotent.
learnings:
  - Swift 6 @MainActor isolation prevents default parameter values that construct actor-isolated types — must pass explicitly from actor-isolated callers
  - 8 parallel builder agents can implement a cohesive feature set without conflicts when given non-overlapping file scopes
  - JSONL-over-filesystem is a surprisingly robust IPC mechanism — no serialization format issues, no connection management, just append and read
  - The fire-and-forget pattern for optional infrastructure (POST to Sentinel, catch and ignore) keeps the critical path clean
  - TypeScript unknown type in JSX requires != null checks, not truthiness checks, to avoid ReactNode assignment errors
